<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Geneva</h1><p class="st-meta">1124 CE – 1535 CE (approx.) · capital Geneva</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The temporal lordship of the bishops of Geneva, whose loss to the citizens and the Reformation turned the city into the 'Protestant Rome' of Calvin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> had been a bishopric since the 4th century. In 1124 the Treaty of Seyssel between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Humbert de Grammont and the Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> confirmed the bishop's lordship over the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> recognised him as an imperial prince in 1162.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled the city through officials, while the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> and later the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a> held the vidomnat, an important judicial office. Citizens gained a charter of liberties from Bishop Adhémar Fabri in 1387. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva's</a> fairs made it a major trading centre in the 15th century. Savoy princes increasingly held the bishopric itself.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-fribourg">Fribourg</a> and Bern in 1526, and in 1533 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-de-la-baume">Pierre de La Baume</a> left the city. In 1535 to 1536 it adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and became an independent republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> arrived in 1536. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a> in 1815.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-geneva.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Geneva" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Original: Werner Luzi for Louis Mühlemann (1977) Vector: Swiss Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canton_of_Geneva.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1124 CE – 1535 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Geneva</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Humbert de Grammont, Adhémar Fabri, Pierre de La Baume</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Franco-Provençal, French, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bishopric of Geneva</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-geneva" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Geneva on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4825 552 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Geneva lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5145 808L5229 694M5145 808L5145 808"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5145 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5145 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5145 808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Geneva. <a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-geneva">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
